    "media_with_text_QUfFa6": {
      "type": "media-with-text",
      "blocks": {
        "heading_MgQgny": {
          "type": "heading",
          "settings": {
            "heading": "Tips for Storing Breast Milk",
            "heading_size": "h3",
            "heading_h1": false
          }
        },
        "text_8h4ebQ": {
          "type": "text",
          "settings": {
            "text": "<p>To get the most out of your breast milk storage, follow these helpful tips:</p><ul><li><strong>Label Your Milk</strong>: Always label the storage container with the date and time of expression. Use the oldest milk first to ensure freshness.</li><li><strong>Storage Duration</strong>: Freshly expressed breast milk can be stored at room temperature for up to 4 hours, in the refrigerator for up to 4 days, and in the freezer for up to 6 months. For best quality, use within 3 months if possible. Previously frozen milk should be used within 24 hours after thawing and should not be refrozen to avoid nutrient breakdown and bacterial growth.</li><li><strong>Safe Thawing</strong>: Thaw frozen breast milk in the refrigerator overnight or by placing the storage bag or bottle in warm water. Never use a microwave to thaw breast milk, as it can create hot spots and damage vital nutrients.</li><li><strong>Handling Tips</strong>: Gently swirl the stored milk before feeding to mix the fat, which may separate during storage. Avoid vigorous shaking to preserve the quality of the milk.</li></ul>",
            "enlarge_text": false
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "heading_MgQgny",
